`]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Which parameters take Muon."})," The blog applies Muon to the hidden 2D weight matrices. The embeddings, the output head (the unembedding), vectors and scalars stay with ",e.jsx(x,{name:"AdamW",children:"AdamW"})," ",e.jsx(a,{id:"jordan2024muon",children:"Jordan et al., 2024"}),". The two groups also differ in state. A Muon matrix keeps one momentum buffer and no second moment ",e.jsx(s,{math:"v_t",display:!1}),", while an AdamW parameter keeps the two moments ",e.jsx(s,{math:"m_t",display:!1})," and ",e.jsx(s,{math:"v_t",display:!1}),". In fp32 that is 4 B of optimizer state per parameter for a Muon matrix against 8 B for an AdamW parameter, so Muon halves the state of the parameters it takes."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"The update's scale."})," The orthogonalized update has its singular values near 1, so its size is set by the matrix's shape, and its root mean square (RMS), the square root of the mean of its squared entries, no longer matches AdamW's. Moonlight rescales each Muon update by ",e.jsx(s,{math:"0.2\\sqrt{\\max(\\text{fan\\_in}, \\text{fan\\_out})}",display:!1}),", where ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"fan_in"})," and ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"fan_out"})," are the weight's input and output widths, its ",e.jsx(s,{math:"n",display:!1})," columns and ",e.jsx(s,{math:"m",display:!1})," rows. This matches the update's RMS to AdamW's, so that the learning rate and weight decay tuned for AdamW carry over to Muon ",e.jsx(a,{id:"liu2025moonlight",children:"Liu et al., 2025"}),". The match is a heuristic for transferring hyperparameters, and it needs checking on the target model."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"What the course adds."})," The code on the slide leaves out the tall-matrix transpose, which the blog's published implementation has. Your ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"Muon"})," keeps the transpose and adds three things to the blog's method: Moonlight's rescale, decoupled weight decay as in AdamW, and Newton–Schulz in fp32. Both groups share one learning rate and one weight decay. The course groups parameters by their role, not their shape. The token embedding and the output head take AdamW although both are 2D. Every other 2D parameter takes Muon, and that includes the bottleneck matrices of latent attention (MLA) from Week 4. The remaining parameters, such as the norm weights, take AdamW."]})]})]}),e.jsxs(i,{title:"Four practices keep Muon stable at scale",accent:6,number:"22",items:"4",children:[e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
