`]}),e.jsx(c,{presetIds:["multistep-v1","cosine-dsv3","cosine-mai"],showBatchTrack:!1}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"Reading the three presets in the schedule builder",children:[e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The builder plots each preset on its own logarithmic learning-rate axis: the multi-step preset in fractions of its peak over percent of the run, because its shape holds for any token count, and the two cosine presets in absolute numbers over tokens."}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Compare the floors first. MAI-Thinking-1's cosine starts to decay at the end of warmup and ends at 10% of its peak ",e.jsx(a,{id:"microsoftai2026maithinking1",children:"Microsoft AI Team, 2026"}),", the same floor that the multi-step schedule reaches with its two cliffs. These two schedules differ only in how they spend the run on the way down. DeepSeek V3's schedule ends lower, at 3.3% of its peak ",e.jsx(a,{id:"deepseekv3-2024",children:"DeepSeek-AI, 2024b"}),", and its long constant phase makes its shape closer to the multi-step schedule's than to MAI's."]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The multi-step schedule holds its peak for 80% of the run and comes down in two steps. DeepSeek V3's holds it for about 68% of the run and then comes down continuously. MAI's leaves the peak at once and spends the whole run on the way down."})]})]}),e.jsx(h,{caption:"MAI-Thinking-1: the rate leaves its peak of 2e-4 right after warmup and falls by cosine to 2e-5 over 30T tokens. It ends at 10% of peak, the floor the multi-step schedule reaches with two cliffs.",children:e.jsx(c,{presetIds:["multistep-v1","cosine-dsv3","cosine-mai"],defaultPreset:"cosine-mai",showBatchTrack:!1})}),e.jsx(h,{caption:"DeepSeek V3: the rate holds 2.2e-4 to 10T tokens, about 68% of the run, falls by cosine to 2.2e-5 and ends at 7.3e-6 for the last 167B tokens, 3.3% of peak. Its long constant phase makes it look more like the multi-step schedule than like MAI's cosine.",children:e.jsx(c,{presetIds:["multistep-v1","cosine-dsv3","cosine-mai"],defaultPreset:"cosine-dsv3",showBatchTrack:!1})}),e.jsxs(i,{title:"WSD separates training from checkpoint decay",accent:4,number:"33",items:"5",children:[e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Cosine: the decay is tied to a fixed run length"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:["Warmup-stable-decay (WSD): named by MiniCPM ",e.jsx(a,{id:"hu2024minicpm",children:"Hu et al., 2024"})]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Stable: constant LR, every checkpoint can start a decay"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Decay: branch a leg when you need one"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:["K2: 2e-4 for 10T tokens, then decay over 5.5T ",e.jsx(a,{id:"kimik2-2025",children:"Kimi Team, 2025"})]}),`
`]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"WSD starts the decay only when a finished model is needed",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["A cosine schedule ties its decay to a run length fixed in advance, so a longer run means starting over. ",e.jsx(x,{name:"WSD",children:"Warmup-stable-decay (WSD)"})," takes the reuse argument to its end. MiniCPM named the schedule ",e.jsx(a,{id:"hu2024minicpm",children:"Hu et al., 2024"}),". ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Warmup"})," ramps the learning rate to its working value. ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Stable"})," trains at that constant rate, and every checkpoint saved in this phase can later start a decay. ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Decay"})," lowers the rate, and it is run as a branch: when you need a finished checkpoint, you take a stable checkpoint and decay a copy of it, while the original stable run continues at its constant rate. The stable run commits to no end point, and each decay leg sets its own horizon."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Kimi K2's main run used this schedule. After a 500-step warmup it trained at a constant 2e-4 for 10T tokens, then decayed along a cosine from 2e-4 to 2e-5 over 5.5T tokens, which ends the 15.5T-token main run ",e.jsx(a,{id:"kimik2-2025",children:"Kimi Team, 2025"}),". A later long-context extension anneals the rate from 2e-5 to 7e-6 ",e.jsx(a,{id:"kimik2-2025",children:"Kimi Team, 2025"}),". That anneal is a separate phase, and its 7e-6 is not the endpoint of the main run."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The course's schedule, ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"wsd_with_warmup"}),", has the same three phases with a different decay. It warms up linearly over ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"warmup_steps"}),", holds the full rate up to and including step ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"decay_start_frac * max_steps"}),", and then falls linearly to ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"min_lr_frac"})," times the peak at ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"max_steps"}),". The Week 5 configs set ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"min_lr_frac: 0"}),", so the course decays linearly to zero, where K2's decay is a cosine to 2e-5."]})]})]}),e.jsxs(i,{title:"Branch a decay leg without ending the stable run",accent:4,number:"34",children:[e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Solid: K2's reported 15.5T run"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:["Dashed: illustrative decay legs, each 10% of its stable span ",e.jsx(a,{id:"hu2024minicpm",children:"Hu et al., 2024"})]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Open question: does skipping decay help fine-tuning?"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:["Yano et al.: one lab, 1B and 8B, SFT only ",e.jsx(a,{id:"yano2026wso",children:"Yano et al., 2026"})]}),`
`]}),e.jsx(c,{presetIds:["multistep-v1","cosine-dsv3","wsd-k2"],defaultPreset:"wsd-k2",showBatchTrack:!1}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"One stable run yields several finished checkpoints",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The builder opens on K2's schedule. The solid curve is K2's reported 15.5T-token main run, which ends at 2e-5. The dashed curves are illustrative, and K2 reports none of them. Each starts from a stable checkpoint, at 5T, 7.5T and 9T tokens, and decays over 10% of the stable tokens before it, so the legs end at 5.5T, 8.25T and 9.9T ",e.jsx(a,{id:"hu2024minicpm",children:"Hu et al., 2024"}),": three finished checkpoints from one stable run that keeps training."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["WSD's case is the reuse of the stable prefix, not a lower final loss. MiniCPM's small models matched or beat cosine at the same step ",e.jsx(a,{id:"hu2024minicpm",children:"Hu et al., 2024"}),". Kimi K3, after separate hyperparameter searches for each schedule, found cosine's final loss consistently lower and chose it ",e.jsx(a,{id:"moonshotai2026kimik3",children:"Kimi Team, 2026"}),". The two results come from different scales and protocols."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Every ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"checkpoint_interval"})," steps, the course trainer writes ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"checkpoint-step-{step}.pt"})," with the model's weights, the optimizer's state, the step count and the number of training windows drawn so far. A decay leg resumes from such a file and continues the data where the checkpoint stopped. The stable run that wrote the file is untouched by the leg."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["One question about decay is open. Could leaving out the learning-rate decay make a model better suited to supervised fine-tuning, even when its pretraining loss is worse? Yano et al. report this result in an ICLR 2026 paper from a single lab, for 1B and 8B models, evaluated with supervised fine-tuning (SFT) only ",e.jsx(a,{id:"yano2026wso",children:"Yano et al., 2026"}),". That is enough to raise the question, and not enough to recommend skipping decay."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["A second lab, Aleph Alpha, has since reported the same reversal at a larger scale. Maskey et al. took one mixture-of-experts run with 30B total and 3B active parameters to 7.5T tokens and compared three endings: a constant learning rate, a cooldown to 10% of the peak learning rate over the last 800B tokens, and a merge of 20 checkpoints from the constant phase ",e.jsx(a,{id:"maskey2026goodpretraining",children:"Maskey et al., 2026"}),". The cooldown beat the constant rate on every pretraining signal, with a validation loss of 1.648 against 1.734, though the merge scored highest on the pretraining aggregate (0.460 against 0.440). After identical mid-training, long-context training and SFT, the cooldown scored worst, with an aggregate score of 0.247 against 0.360 for the constant rate and 0.363 for the merge ",e.jsx(a,{id:"maskey2026goodpretraining",children:"Maskey et al., 2026"}),'. The authors name their limit as "one 30B MoE family with one seed per setup". Kolibri and Ling 2.6 now end pretraining without a decay and merge checkpoints instead, a "Warmup-Stable-Merge" schedule (',e.jsx(a,{id:"alephalpha2026kolibri",paren:!1,children:"Aleph Alpha, 2026"}),"; ",e.jsx(a,{id:"lingteam2026ling26",paren:!1,children:"Ling Team, 2026"}),'). Nemotron 3 Ultra merges stable-phase checkpoints "weighted to emulate our learning rate decay schedule" for evaluation ',e.jsx(a,{id:"nvidia2026nemotron3ultra",children:"NVIDIA, 2026"}),"."]})]})]}),e.jsxs(i,{title:"Batch size changes through training too",reading:!0,accent:5,number:"35",children:[e.jsx(s,{math:"B \\to kB \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\eta \\to \\sqrt{k}\\,\\eta \\qquad \\text{(Adam)}",label:"(7)"}),e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Bigger batch: less wall-clock time, less learning per token"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Critical batch size (CBS): where returns start to diminish"}),`
