`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:["DeepSeek V3: 3,072 to 15,360 sequences over the first 469B tokens ",e.jsx(a,{id:"deepseekv3-2024",children:"DeepSeek-AI, 2024b"})]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:["AI2: double the batch when the measured CBS allows ",e.jsx(a,{id:"merrill2025cbs",children:"Merrill et al., 2025"})]}),`
`]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"Three batch strategies and what each one shows",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["DeepSeek LLM fits a law to a compute budget. After tuning proxy runs it reported ",e.jsx(s,{math:"B_{\\mathrm{opt}} = 0.2920\\, C^{0.3271}",display:!1})," and ",e.jsx(s,{math:"\\eta_{\\mathrm{opt}} = 0.3118\\, C^{-0.1250}",display:!1}),", which choose the batch size and the learning rate together ",e.jsx(a,{id:"deepseekv1-2024",children:"DeepSeek-AI, 2024a"}),". Here ",e.jsx(s,{math:"C",display:!1})," is the training compute in FLOPs and ",e.jsx(s,{math:"B_{\\mathrm{opt}}",display:!1})," is the batch size in tokens. These are empirical fits, and the coefficients have meaning only in those units. The report fit them on runs from about 1e17 to 2e19 FLOPs and checked the result at 1e20 FLOPs. Keep the units and coefficients as reported, and do not extend the fit beyond the measured range."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["DeepSeek V3 ramps the batch by training progress. Its batch grows from 3,072 to 15,360 sequences over the first 469B tokens and stays at 15,360 for the rest of training, so the run uses smaller, efficient batches early and more parallelism later ",e.jsx(a,{id:"deepseekv3-2024",children:"DeepSeek-AI, 2024b"}),". The report does not test the ramp on its own."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["AI2 warms the batch up by the measured CBS. It doubles the batch whenever the measured CBS reaches twice the current batch and scales the learning rate by ",e.jsx(s,{math:"\\sqrt{2}",display:!1})," at each doubling. In a branched comparison at a fixed token budget, this matched the loss of a fixed-batch run with 43% fewer serial steps ",e.jsx(a,{id:"merrill2025cbs",children:"Merrill et al., 2025"}),". Batch-size warmup is the practical use of the CBS curve: small batches early, while the CBS is tiny, and large batches later, once it has grown."]})]})]}),e.jsxs(i,{title:"Batch warmup: 2026 reports disagree",reading:!0,accent:6,number:"38",items:"4",children:[e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:["Qwen3.8, Muon: no gain, 18.8% more steps ",e.jsx(a,{id:"qwen2026qwen38flashnext",children:"Qwen Team, 2026"})]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:["DeepSeek V4.1: fixed batch of 100.6M tokens, no instability ",e.jsx(a,{id:"deepseekai2026deepseekv41",children:"DeepSeek-AI, 2026b"})]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:["Mistral Large 4, AdamW: ramps 1× to 4×, for throughput ",e.jsx(a,{id:"mistral2026large4",children:"Mistral AI, 2026"})]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:["A.X K2, AdamW: ramp against early gradient spikes ",e.jsx(a,{id:"baek2026axk2",children:"Baek et al., 2026"})]}),`
`]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"Four 2026 reports split on batch warmup",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Batch-size warmup starts with a small batch and grows it, on the argument that the critical batch size is small early in training. Four labs split on it in 2026. Qwen3.8-Flash-Next retested the ramp under Muon on a mixture-of-experts model with 10.8B total and 0.89B active parameters, trained on 4T tokens. The ramp grew the batch from 6.3M to 25.2M tokens by 524B tokens, once with the constant-batch run's peak learning rate and once with a lower one. Both ramps ended within run-to-run variance of the constant batch, but slightly worse. They needed 18.8% more optimizer steps and changed no stability measure, and Qwen concludes that the warmup is no longer necessary ",e.jsx(a,{id:"qwen2026qwen38flashnext",children:"Qwen Team, 2026"}),". Its reasons are the optimizer and the experts: under Muon, a batch below the predicted optimum costs more than one above it, Muon keeps its data efficiency at larger batches where AdamW's degrades, and a large batch gives every expert enough tokens ",e.jsx(a,{id:"qwen2026qwen38flashnext",children:"Qwen Team, 2026"}),". DeepSeek ramped V4-Flash's batch to 75.5M tokens ",e.jsx(a,{id:"deepseekai2026deepseekv4",children:"DeepSeek-AI, 2026a"}),'. It then trained V4.1-Flash on 45T tokens at a fixed batch of 100.6M tokens "with no instability", without saying why it changed ',e.jsx(a,{id:"deepseekai2026deepseekv41",children:"DeepSeek-AI, 2026b"}),"."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Mistral Large 4, trained with AdamW, ramps its batch from 1× to 4× its predicted batch size. It argues that a large batch early in training wastes compute and tokens, because a gradient estimated from a fraction of the batch suffices, and that the ramp also acts as a regularizer, more so the more often the data repeats. A ramp from 0.25× to 2× performed best in its experiments, and it chose 1× to 4× for wall-clock time, because larger batches use the hardware more efficiently ",e.jsx(a,{id:"mistral2026large4",children:"Mistral AI, 2026"}),'. A.X K2, also trained with AdamW, ramps its batch over roughly its first 295B tokens "to mitigate gradient spikes" ',e.jsx(a,{id:"baek2026axk2",children:"Baek et al., 2026"}),". Neither report publishes numbers for its ramp. The one measured test ran under Muon, so treat the optimizer as one possible reason for the split, not as a settled rule."]})]})]})]}),`
