`]}),e.jsx(g,{showGradientPanel:!0,projectionLayout:!0}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"Reading the loss-scaling panel",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The widget has two parts. On the left, the bit layouts of the five formats are drawn to scale, so you can see that bf16 shares fp32's exponent width. On the right, a synthetic histogram of gradient magnitudes, a bell shape in log space peaking near ",e.jsx(s,{math:"10^{-5}",display:!1}),", spans ",e.jsx(s,{math:"10^{-12}",display:!1})," to ",e.jsx(s,{math:"10^{2}",display:!1})," on a logarithmic axis. Dashed lines mark the chosen format's thresholds: at the low end the RNE round-to-zero midpoint, the minimum subnormal and the minimum normal, and for fp16 at the high end its maximum finite value, 65,504. Bars at or below the midpoint turn red, bars above the maximum take the overflow color, and two readouts give each group's share of the mass."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The loss-scale slider runs from ",e.jsx(s,{math:"s = 2^0",display:!1})," to ",e.jsx(s,{math:"s = 2^{16}",display:!1}),". Each doubling shifts every bar right by ",e.jsx(s,{math:"\\log_{10} 2 \\approx 0.3",display:!1})," decades, since ",e.jsx(s,{math:"\\log_{10}(2x) = \\log_{10} x + \\log_{10} 2",display:!1}),", while the thresholds stay where they are. The scale moves the gradients relative to fixed thresholds, so the red share falls as the scale grows. In fp16 about 12.6% of the illustrative mass rounds to zero at a scale of 1, and none from ",e.jsx(s,{math:"2^{15}",display:!1})," on. The same shift moves the top of the histogram toward fp16's maximum: up to ",e.jsx(s,{math:"2^{9}",display:!1})," no mass lies above 65,504, from ",e.jsx(s,{math:"2^{10}",display:!1})," on the overflow readout is no longer zero, and at ",e.jsx(s,{math:"2^{16}",display:!1})," it reads about 1.9%."]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The format toggle does the opposite. Switching to bf16 leaves the histogram where it was and moves the thresholds. bf16's round-to-zero midpoint, about 4.6e-41, lies more than 26 decades left of the axis, and its maximum, about 3.4e38, lies far to the right. No illustrated gradient underflows or overflows at any scale. The panel models these two range limits only."})]})]}),e.jsx(h,{caption:"fp16 at a loss scale of 2¹⁶ = 65,536, the course scaler's starting value. Every bar lies about 4.8 decades further right than in the opening state, and the thresholds stay where they were. No mass rounds to zero now, but 1.9% lies above fp16's maximum of 65,504 and would overflow.",children:e.jsx(g,{showGradientPanel:!0,projectionLayout:!0,initialLog2Scale:16})}),e.jsx(h,{caption:"bf16 at a loss scale of 1: the histogram has not moved, and both readouts show 0.0%. Where fp16 lost 12.6% of the mass at this scale, bf16 loses none, because its round-to-zero midpoint, about 4.6e-41, and its maximum, about 3.4e38, lie far off both ends of the axis.",children:e.jsx(g,{showGradientPanel:!0,projectionLayout:!0,initialPanelFormat:"bf16"})}),e.jsxs(i,{title:"FP8 needs local scaling and high-precision islands",accent:4,number:"56",items:"6",children:[e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
